# The accessor of a constant whose value a header holds, and what stands
# around it (design.md §4.19; panel 038; CLAUDE.md §7). Split out of
# `emit/body.hero` on 2026-10-10, when defects 570 and 571 gave the accessor
# lines of its own and that file passed §11's 300 lines: a body has a
# prologue and blocks, an accessor has neither.
#
# **The accessor is the compiler's line among the program's**, the one place
# in the definitions where a group's macro is expanded on purpose: its
# `return NAME;` (or, for a record's constant, the initialisation
# `emit/record_constant.hero` writes) names the header's value. So a
# `deprecated` attribute is quiet over it and spoken again after it
# (`emit/deprecation.hero`, defect 571), and every check of a binding is
# raised again after it, since a `_Pragma` in the value reaches every line
# below (`emit/checks_raised.hero`, defect 570). A constant of the
# compiler's own headers, the library's `HERO_OS_OK` among them, is the
# runtime's, and is written with neither.

use ast
use source
use check/state
use ir/containers
use emit/ctype
use emit/signature
use emit/record_constant
use emit/checks_raised
use emit/deprecation
use emit/writer

# The accessor, its signature at the declaration as every signature is.
function accessor(@w: writer.Writer, f: containers.Function, c: state.Checked, names: ctype.Names, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    grouped = deprecation.group_constant(s, decls, f)

    if grouped
        deprecation.say(@w, line: deprecation.QUIET)
    writer.at_span(@w, s, offset: f.span.start)

    # A record's constant is built from the header's value, which may be a
    # brace list that `return` cannot take (defect 094).
    if record_constant.is_record_value(c, decls, f)
        record_constant.accessor(@w, f, c, names, s, decls)
    else
        writer.line(@w, text: signature.signature(f, c, names, s, decls) + " {")
        writer.at_generated(@w)
        writer.line(@w, text: "    return " + source.span_text(s, decls[f.decl].name) + ";")
        writer.line(@w, text: "}")

    if grouped
        deprecation.say(@w, line: deprecation.SPOKEN)
    checks_raised.after_constant(@w, s, decls, f)
